        ! Seeded at the calibrated value. From 340 the first step subtracted
        ! adjuster*(96.0-7.5) = 885, which is 2.6 times the parameter itself, so it
        ! was clamped to its floor of 0.01 and then spent five iterations climbing
        ! back. The step is now bounded relatively as well, below.
        ! ALEPH IS SEEDED WHERE THE TARGET PUTS IT, not where the old units did.
        ! At 143.06 the model produced a value of life of 1,068 times average
        ! labour income against a target of 80, and the trust region halves aleph
        ! at most once an iteration, so four iterations were spent walking down a
        ! ladder whose bottom is known: the ratio is close to linear in aleph, so
        ! 80/1068 of 143.06 is about 11. Every block downstream of the risk term
        ! was reading a model in which dying was worth thirteen times what the
        ! calibration says, which is why drug quantities sat on their floor.
        real(8) :: value_of_life=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*11.0d0+perceived_probability_lower*120
        real(8) :: value_of_life_step_1=0
        
        real(8) :: max_initial_endowment=7
        real(8) :: max_initial_endowment_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_max_initial_endowment
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_max_initial_endowment=10.d0**(-3)
        
        ! WARM START. These three are the values the calibration of 16 August
        ! converged to, and starting from them saves roughly fifteen equilibrium
        ! solves on every restart. They are starting points, not settings: all
        ! three still iterate. The proper fix is to write the calibrated values to
        ! a file at the end of each loop and read them at startup, so a killed run
        ! resumes instead of beginning again; that is not done yet, and this run
        ! having been killed once is what made it worth doing at all.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness=(1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.304+perceived_probability_lower*0.3
        
        ! ON, AND IT WAS OFF. At zero the update below is size_homelessness minus
        ! zero times the gap, so the parameter never moved and the homelessness
        ! target was reached by hand between runs instead. That was workable while
        ! the rental grid was stable. It is not now: taking the mortgage
        ! eligibility overwrite out of housing_size moved the smallest rental, so
        ! the hand set 0.21 leaves homelessness at 0.007 per cent against a target
        ! of 0.399 and no iteration closes it.
        !
        ! The sign is already right. size_homelessness scales the service flow of
        ! the street, so raising it raises homelessness, and the update subtracts
        ! the adjuster times (model minus data), which rises when the model is
        ! below target. 20 moves it about 0.08 per iteration at the current gap,
        ! which crosses the [0,1] range in roughly a dozen equilibrium solves.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_adjuster=20

        ! AND THE TRAVERSE HAS TO DAMP, for aleph's reason. The adjuster is
        ! twenty and the gap is a rate, so a gap of 0.031 moves the parameter by
        ! 0.62 on a parameter bounded in [0,1]: it went 0.531 to 0 in one step,
        ! then 0 to 0.0798, then back to 0. Homelessness is the paper's headline
        ! moment and it was being read off a parameter slamming between its
        ! bounds.
        !
        ! The bracket cannot rescue it yet. A bracket whose endpoints were priced
        ! at different values of life is correctly discarded, aleph is still
        ! moving twenty per cent a loop against a five per cent tolerance, so the
        ! bracket is thrown away every loop and the traverse is all there is.
        ! Damping the traverse is what makes the traverse usable in the meantime,
        ! and it costs nothing once the bracket does survive.
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_adjuster_start=20
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_damping=0.5
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_growth=1.2
        real(8), parameter :: size_homelessness_adjuster_floor=10.d0**(-2)
        real(8) :: gap_size_homelessness_1=0,previous_gap_size_homelessness=0
        integer :: have_gap_size_homelessness=0
        real(8) :: distance_size_homelessness
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_size_homelessness=10.d0**(-3)

        ! A BRACKET, BECAUSE THE LINEAR STEP CANNOT LAND ON THE TARGET.
        !
        ! Homelessness is very nearly a step function of size_homelessness, since
        ! the housing choice is discrete and the marginal households jump together.
        ! Measured over the thirteen iterations of the run archived in
        ! scratchpad/cal_run/stale_state: at 0.3836 the model gives 0.0 per cent,
        ! at 0.4634 it gives 1.22, at 0.3788 it gives 10.9 and at 0.5586 it gives
        ! 34.5, against a target of 0.399. So the parameter has to be placed inside
        ! a window narrower than the adjuster's own step.
        !
        ! It could not be. A gap of one percentage point moves the parameter by
        ! 20*0.01 = 0.2, which is wider than the whole window, so the update
        ! overshot every time it crossed: it slammed to the zero clamp, ratcheted
        ! back up in exact steps of 0.0798 while the model read zero, crossed the
        ! threshold, overshot to zero again, and never converged. The adjuster is
        ! not wrong, it is a range traverser, which is what the comment above says
        ! it was sized for.
        !
        ! So the step now only runs until there is a bracket, and after that the
        ! interval is bisected. lo is the largest value known to leave the model
        ! BELOW target, hi the smallest known to leave it at or above, which is
        ! well defined because homelessness rises in size_homelessness: a better
        ! street puts more households on it. Negative means not found yet.
        !
        ! The bracket is deliberately NOT written to the warm start file. The
        ! calibration state is read back positionally, so adding fields to it is a
        ! format change, and losing the bracket on a resume costs two iterations to
        ! rebuild whereas a misread field would be silent and wrong.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_lo=-1
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_hi=-1

        ! AND THE BRACKET HAS TO BE ABLE TO THROW ITSELF AWAY.
        !
        ! Bisecting assumes the moment is a function of this parameter alone. It
        ! is not, because every other parameter moves in the same outer loop, and
        ! one of them moves enormously: over the four iterations that formed the
        ! first bracket the value of life went 0.01, 80.96, 133.41, 168.27, 192.86.
        ! A household's willingness to live on a street that kills it is not
        ! comparable across a change like that, so the two endpoints were priced
        ! under different models and the interval between them means nothing.
        !
        ! It showed up as the moment moving the wrong way. Measured: 0.3836 gave
        ! 0.00 per cent, 0.4634 gave 1.22, then the midpoint 0.4235 gave 20.56 and
        ! the next midpoint 0.4036 gave 32.11. Lowering the parameter raised
        ! homelessness twice in a row, which cannot happen if a better street puts
        ! more households on it. A bracket that ignores this converges neatly onto
        ! a stale endpoint and reports a calibrated parameter that was never
        ! calibrated against anything.
        !
        ! So each endpoint now carries the moment measured there, and a new
        ! evaluation that contradicts monotonicity discards the bracket and goes
        ! back to traversing. The pattern is the one the labour supply search
        ! already uses: when the answer lands outside the bracket, the bracket was
        ! wrong, so throw it away rather than trusting it.
        !
        ! This makes the update honest, not convergent. If the violations keep
        ! coming, the message they carry is that this parameter cannot be
        ! calibrated while the value of life is still moving under it, and the
        ! answer to that is to sequence the loop rather than to bisect harder.
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_lo_moment=-1
        real(8) :: size_homelessness_hi_moment=-1

        ! The monotonicity check above catches a stale bracket only once it has
        ! already produced a contradictory evaluation, which costs an equilibrium
        ! solve to learn. The cause is known, so it can be tested for directly:
        ! a bracket is valid only if its two endpoints were priced under the same
        ! value of life. This records aleph when the bracket is opened, and any
        ! evaluation arriving after aleph has moved by more than the tolerance
        ! throws the bracket away before it is used.
        !
        ! Hard sequencing, holding this parameter fixed until aleph converges, is
        ! the other way to get the same guarantee and it is NOT what is done here.
        ! The two are jointly determined: the value of life is measured over the
        ! whole distribution, the homeless included, and the street is what makes
        ! dying cheap. Freezing one to converge the other can settle on a
        ! different fixed point, or on none. Refusing to trust a bracket that
        ! spans two different models is the weaker claim and the safe one.
        real(8) :: value_of_life_at_bracket=-1
        real(8), parameter :: bracket_value_of_life_tolerance=0.05
        
        integer, parameter :: employment_grid_size=9
        integer, parameter :: productivity_below_which_to_reduce_ss=9
        real(8) :: scaling_of_ss=(1-reduce_ss_by_half)*1+reduce_ss_by_half*0.5
        real(8) :: scaling_of_ss_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_scaling_of_ss
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_scaling_of_ss=10.d0**(-3)
        
        real(8), parameter :: increased_risk_of_death_homeless=0.6 
        ! THIS IS THE MULTIPLIER MINUS ONE, not the multiplier. Survival is built
        ! as phi - k*(1 - phi) at line 16620ish, so the death probability comes out
        ! (1 + k) times the baseline. At k = 6.9 the effective mortality rate ratio
        ! was 7.9, which is not what the written model says and not what the
        ! stylized model's kappa was derived from. 5.9 gives an effective 6.9,
        ! which is Hjorthoj's ratio and is what both models now use. The LEVEL is
        ! calibrated in the second stage inside the Degenhardt range, so this is a
        ! starting value; what matters is that the units are now the same in the
        ! code and in the note.
        real(8) :: increased_risk_of_death_addicted=&
            have_increased_death_from_addiction*5.9+(1-have_increased_death_from_addiction)*size_of_penalty*5
        real(8) :: increased_risk_of_death_homeless_addicted=0

        ! THE MORTALITY MULTIPLIERS VARY WITH AGE, AND THE DATA FOR IT WAS
        ! ALREADY IN THE REPOSITORY AND UNREAD.
        !
        ! data/us_mortality_multipliers.csv has sixty rows, ages 26 to 85, with
        ! shape_sud, the CDC drug poisoning profile normalised to one at its
        ! peak, and rr_homeless, Baggett's all cause ratio by age. The code read
        ! neither file and used two flat excesses instead.
        !
        ! Equation (25) of the note:
        !
        !   kappa_phi_d(j) = 1 + (kappa_bar_phi_d - 1) * shape_sud(j)
        !   kappa_phi_H(j) = max{1, RR(j) / [(1-s) + s*kappa_phi_d(j)]}
        !
        ! with s the SUD share of the homeless, because Baggett's ratio is
        ! measured on a population that mostly also carries a SUD and using it
        ! raw would credit housing with a mortality effect that belongs to the
        ! disorder.
        !
        ! Survival is then MULTIPLICATIVE in the two, as the note has it. The old
        ! code added the two excesses, which is a different and larger number
        ! whenever both are large, and both are large exactly for the households
        ! this paper is about.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: shape_sud_profile=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: rr_homeless_profile=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: kappa_phi_d_by_age=1
        ! SMI prevalence by age in the model, against the profile the onset
        ! identity is built to reproduce. The aggregate cannot say whether a
        ! shortfall is the newborn draw failing to arrive or the differential
        ! mortality of SMI households eroding it with age, and those call for
        ! opposite fixes. Read at age one it is the draw; read as a slope against
        ! the profile it is the mortality.
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_smi_mass_by_age=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: model_mass_by_age=0
        real(8), dimension(life_span) :: kappa_phi_h_by_age=1
        ! ASSIGNED, at the midpoint of the literature range. All cause standardised
        ! mortality ratios for opioid use disorder run 6 to 10, so 8 is the middle
        ! of it rather than an old point estimate. This used to be calibrated on
        ! the drug price participation elasticity; that elasticity is now an
        ! untargeted validation moment instead.
        real(8) :: kappa_phi_d_level=8.0
        real(8), parameter :: sud_share_of_homeless=0.217
        integer :: mortality_profiles_loaded=0
                            
        ! UNEMPLOYMENT INSURANCE IS NOW CERTAIN FOR THE FIRST YEAR OF A SPELL
        ! and pays nothing after it. This removes a parameter rather than a
        ! state: a household in its first year of unemployment and one in its
        ! third still have different income, the replacement rate against zero,
        ! and different continuation values, so the two do not merge. The three
        ! employment states stay, and with them the long spells that are what
        ! actually cost a household its housing.
        real(8), parameter :: probability_of_ui=1.0

        ! UI DURATION. With this at one an unbroken unemployment spell is
        ! insured in its first period only and pays nothing after, which is what
        ! the paper describes and what employment_transition's own comment
        ! claimed it already did. At zero the spell stays in whichever state it
        ! entered, so UI runs for as long as the spell does; that was the
        ! behaviour before, and it is kept switchable because the two differ a
        ! great deal in how costly the disorder's unemployment risk is.
        integer, parameter :: ui_first_period_only=1
                
        real(8) :: utility_improvement=include_addiction*0
        real(8) :: utility_improvement_adjuster=0
        real(8) :: distance_utility_improvement
        real(8) :: stopping_rule_utility_improvement=10.d0**(-3)
        
        real(8), dimension(2) :: ss_decrease_addicted=ss_decrease_for_retired_addicted*&
            include_addiction*(/0,0/)
        
        ! REMOVED FROM THE MODEL. The written budget constraint has no consumption tax.
        ! It was already zero, so nothing changes numerically, but it is kept at
        ! zero as a parameter rather than deleted because it appears in a dozen
        ! expressions and threading it out of all of them risks more than it
        ! gains. Anything that reads it reads a hard zero.
        real(8), parameter :: consumption_tax=0
        real(8) :: max_food_cost=&
            ((1-perceived_probability_lower)*0.126+perceived_probability_lower*0.132)/(1+consumption_tax)
        ! THE BENEFIT REDUCTION RATE, the statutory SNAP taper. The benefit is
        ! the maximum allotment less thirty cents per dollar of net income
        ! rather than a top up to a hard floor. It used to be a hard floor,
        ! which is not the programme and, more to the point, is not a rule the
        ! stylized model of Section 2 can carry: a hard floor gives every
        ! household below it the SAME income, so one atom of households crosses
        ! the homelessness margin together and the rate jumps from zero to 1.72
        ! per cent with nothing in between, never taking the 0.399 the model is
        ! calibrated to. With the taper, resources after the transfer are
        ! strictly increasing in income and no atom forms. The two models use
        ! the same rule.
        real(8), parameter :: food_stamp_taper=0.30d0
                                                                        
