        ! ASSET GRID, and the one number here that is a judgement rather than a
        ! deduction. The a' search is QUADRATIC in this, so ninety to fifty takes the
        ! renter loop from 91 points to 51 and is worth about 3.2x, more than
        ! anything else left on the list.
        !
        ! The reason for caution is that the objective is not single peaked in
        ! a', and the things that break it, voucher eligibility as a step in
        ! income and the food stamp floor, are where the welfare comparison
        ! happens at margins of tenths of a per cent. What makes seventy
        ! defensible is that the spacing is dense near zero and sparse at the
        ! top, so the points removed are the ones above the wealth distribution's
        ! upper decile, where few households sit and where the only notch,
        ! capital income pushing a household over the eligibility threshold, is
        ! already coarsely resolved.
        !
        ! IT IS ONE LINE TO PUT BACK, and it must be validated before any
        ! reported number rests on it: solve the benchmark at seventy and at
        ! ninety and compare the targeted moments and the two policy
        ! counterfactuals, not just the value function.
        ! FORTY, NOT FIFTY, and this is the one cut in the whole exercise that is
        ! a judgement rather than a deduction. The a' search is quadratic in the
        ! grid, so forty against fifty is 1.56x, which is what takes the
        ! calibration under a day once mental illness doubles the state space:
        ! thirty hours becomes nineteen on four cores, about two on forty.
        !
        ! IT MUST BE VALIDATED, and the validation is cheap now that the owner
        ! branch is gone: solve the benchmark at forty and at fifty and compare
        ! the targeted moments and BOTH counterfactuals, not just the value
        ! function. The reason to be careful is specific rather than general. The
        ! objective is not single peaked in a': voucher eligibility is a step
        ! function of income which includes r*a, the food stamp floor kinks, and
        ! housing is discrete. Coarsening this grid coarsens exactly the dimension
        ! that carries the notches.
        integer, parameter :: positive_financial_grid_size=40
        integer, parameter :: negative_financial_grid_size=61
        
        integer, parameter :: grid_gaps=coarse_grid*10+(1-coarse_grid)
           
        integer, parameter :: financial_grid_size=positive_financial_grid_size+negative_financial_grid_size
                                          
        real(8), dimension(financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid
        real(8), dimension(negative_financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid_distance_negative
        real(8), dimension(financial_grid_size) :: financial_grid_distance_positive
                
        ! HOMEOWNERSHIP IS OUT OF THE MODEL. The revision is a renter model: the
        ! written model has four housing statuses, homelessness and three rental
        ! sizes, no mortgages, and assets that cannot go negative. Rather than
        ! delete two and a half thousand lines of owner and mortgage code, which
        ! would be irreversible and hard to check, ownership is switched off at
        ! the three places that let a household reach it.
        !
        ! THIS IS THE FIRST. has_house is set from years_left_on_mortgage_index at
